          "For residential homes, the sales comparison (market) approach — adjusting recent comparable sales — is the primary method."),
        q(27, .valuation, "When adjusting comparables in the sales comparison approach, the appraiser:",
          ["Adjusts the subject to match each comp", "Adjusts each comp toward the subject", "Averages all sale prices", "Uses only the highest comp"], 1,
          "The subject is never adjusted; each comparable is adjusted (add for features the comp lacks, subtract for features the comp has extra) toward the subject."),
        q(28, .valuation, "The cost approach is most useful for valuing:",
          ["A 30-year-old tract home", "A special-purpose property like a church or school", "Vacant farmland", "An apartment complex"], 1,
          "The cost approach (land value + replacement cost − depreciation) is best for unique, special-purpose properties with few comparable sales."),
        q(29, .valuation, "The income approach converts a property's expected income into value primarily using:",
          ["Replacement cost", "A capitalization rate", "A gross living area adjustment", "An assessment ratio"], 1,
          "The income approach divides net operating income by a capitalization rate (Value = NOI ÷ Cap Rate) to estimate value of income property."),
        q(30, .valuation, "A property has net operating income of $30,000 and the market cap rate is 10%. Its indicated value is:",
          ["$3,000", "$30,000", "$300,000", "$3,000,000"], 2,
          "Value = NOI ÷ Cap Rate = $30,000 ÷ 0.10 = $300,000."),
        q(31, .valuation, "The Gross Rent Multiplier (GRM) is calculated as:",
          ["Sale price ÷ monthly (or annual) rent", "NOI ÷ cap rate", "Rent ÷ expenses", "Price ÷ square feet"], 0,
          "GRM = Sale Price ÷ Gross Rent; multiplying a subject's rent by a market GRM gives a quick value estimate."),
        q(32, .valuation, "Loss in value from outdated design or features within the property is which type of depreciation?",
          ["Physical deterioration", "Functional obsolescence", "External (economic) obsolescence", "Accrued appreciation"], 1,
          "Functional obsolescence is a loss in value from outdated or poorly designed features inside the property (e.g., one bathroom, awkward floor plan)."),
        q(33, .valuation, "A new airport built next to a neighborhood lowers home values. This is:",
          ["Physical deterioration", "Functional obsolescence", "External obsolescence", "Curable depreciation"], 2,
          "External (economic) obsolescence is a loss in value caused by factors outside the property's boundaries and is generally incurable."),
        q(34, .valuation, "The appraisal principle that a property's maximum value is set by the cost of acquiring an equally desirable substitute is:",
          ["Conformity", "Substitution", "Contribution", "Anticipation"], 1,
          "The principle of substitution holds that a buyer will pay no more than the cost of an equally desirable substitute property."),
        q(35, .valuation, "Reconciliation in an appraisal refers to:",
          ["Averaging the three approaches", "Weighing the approaches to arrive at a final value", "Recording the appraisal", "Adjusting for inflation"], 1,
          "Reconciliation is the appraiser's judgment-weighted analysis of the approaches' results to arrive at a single final value estimate (never a simple average)."),
        q(36, .valuation, "An over-improvement (e.g., a luxury kitchen in a modest neighborhood) illustrates the appraisal principle of:",
          ["Regression", "Progression", "Anticipation", "Change"], 0,
          "Regression: a higher-value property is dragged down by surrounding lower-value properties; progression is the reverse."),
    ]

